00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:34.030
but with their much smaller population,

00:09:34.030 --> 00:09:37.730
that equates to 15 pies
per person every year.

00:09:37.730 --> 00:09:38.592
- What?

00:09:38.592 --> 00:09:39.425
- Oh!

00:09:39.425 --> 00:09:41.840
15 per year for the Kiwis.

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:43.160
- Why are they called Kiwis?

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:45.144
We should be calling them Pie-e's.

00:09:45.144 --> 00:09:46.110
(laughing)

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:49.040
- Funny that we're mentioning
Australian savory pies

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:52.230
because the next pie is actually inspired-

00:09:52.230 --> 00:09:53.063
- In-pie-red?

00:09:53.063 --> 00:09:54.570
- By Australian savory pie.

00:09:54.570 --> 00:09:57.190
We're seeing Chef Ryan at Chi Spacca.

00:09:57.190 --> 00:10:00.067
We're gonna be trying
their bone marrow pie.

00:10:00.067 --> 00:10:02.883
(lighthearted music)

00:10:02.883 --> 00:10:03.980
- Chi Spacca has been described

00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:06.280
as a "meat speakeasy" by Jonathan Gold.

00:10:06.280 --> 00:10:08.290
This dish represents trying to take

00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:10.070
a classic meat dish,
an Australian meat pie,

00:10:10.070 --> 00:10:12.510
and really trying to do it
the best of its possibilities.

00:10:12.510 --> 00:10:13.950
So, first step is making a veal stock.

00:10:13.950 --> 00:10:16.270
Roasted veal bones, simmering
for about 6 to 8 hours.

00:10:16.270 --> 00:10:18.100
We cut up beef cheek into little pieces,

00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:20.030
and then we salt them
and let it sit overnight.

00:10:20.030 --> 00:10:21.430
That allows for the salt
to actually penetrate

00:10:21.430 --> 00:10:22.270
the meat itself.

00:10:22.270 --> 00:10:23.607
When you have a stew or a braised meat

00:10:23.607 --> 00:10:26.070
and the meat isn't seasoned
ahead of time, you can tell.

00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:28.510
The meat is just being
covered up by a salty liquid.

00:10:28.510 --> 00:10:30.410
You get a nice caramelization
on the outside of it.

00:10:30.410 --> 00:10:31.620
And heavy caramelization.

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:34.460
We then take that out of the
pan and add cipollini onions.

00:10:34.460 --> 00:10:35.657
Cipollini, I mean, it really just means

00:10:35.657 --> 00:10:37.050
"little onion" in Italian.

00:10:37.050 --> 00:10:39.570
And button mushrooms with a
little bit of thyme and garlic.

00:10:39.570 --> 00:10:41.710
So we want to get the same
color on those vegetables

00:10:41.710 --> 00:10:42.543
that we do on the meat.

00:10:42.543 --> 00:10:43.870
The reason we do that separate is because

00:10:43.870 --> 00:10:46.440
they cook at different
temperatures and different speeds.

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:48.860
We de-glaze with the fortified veal stock.

00:10:48.860 --> 00:10:50.040
Bring the meat in and let the meat cook

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:51.200
for about 2 hours or so.

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:53.240
Beef cheek is something
that most people don't use,

00:10:53.240 --> 00:10:55.770
but it works amazing
because you're braising it

00:10:55.770 --> 00:10:57.390
and beef cheek has a
lot of connective tissue

00:10:57.390 --> 00:10:58.850
that breaks down and makes it so that

00:10:58.850 --> 00:11:00.530
you have this beautiful
mouthfeel to the sauce,

00:11:00.530 --> 00:11:01.730
and the meat itself is tender.

00:11:01.730 --> 00:11:03.340
We put the vegetables
in for the last hour.

00:11:03.340 --> 00:11:04.520
If we put the vegetables
in the very beginning,

00:11:04.520 --> 00:11:05.353
they would just dissolve.

00:11:05.353 --> 00:11:07.050
Roll out just a savory pie dough.

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:08.280
In the top pie we cut a little circle

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:09.870
so that the bone marrow
actually can fit through.

00:11:09.870 --> 00:11:11.670
We sell them both baked off,

00:11:11.670 --> 00:11:12.720
like ready to go to eat,

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:14.350
or you can take it home and bake it.

00:11:14.350 --> 00:11:15.533
We've actually probably sold more raw pies

00:11:15.533 --> 00:11:16.850
than we have cooked pies.

00:11:16.850 --> 00:11:17.950
We actually are happy about that,

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:20.240
because when you bake a pie,
you want to eat it right away.

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:22.200
The texture from the bone marrow,

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:24.310
the fattiness of the bone
marrow with the stew,

00:11:24.310 --> 00:11:25.800
it almost sounds like
it can be overwhelming,

00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:27.390
you know, kind of, why do you need that?

00:11:27.390 --> 00:11:28.910
People call bone marrow "meat butter".

00:11:28.910 --> 00:11:30.560
It adds this unctuous texture.

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:33.132
It's really, really delicious.

00:11:33.132 --> 00:11:35.740
(cash register ding)

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:38.260
- Look at this pie!

00:11:38.260 --> 00:11:41.310
- There's something strange yet beautiful

00:11:41.310 --> 00:11:45.260
about a bone protruding from crust.

00:11:45.260 --> 00:11:46.460
- You know what it looks like, right?

00:11:46.460 --> 00:11:48.110
- It looks like some Flintstones (bleep).

00:11:48.110 --> 00:11:50.183
- Oh, I was gonna say it
looks like a bellybutton.

00:11:50.183 --> 00:11:52.120
(laughing)

00:11:52.120 --> 00:11:54.170
- The fact that you bring this one home

00:11:54.170 --> 00:11:56.370
and heat it up so your own house

00:11:56.370 --> 00:11:58.280
can get filled with that aroma.

00:11:58.280 --> 00:11:59.187
- Oh my.

00:11:59.187 --> 00:12:00.680
All right, let's dig in.

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:02.070
Oh my God.

00:12:02.070 --> 00:12:03.400
What?

00:12:03.400 --> 00:12:05.093
Why is it so gooey?

00:12:06.060 --> 00:12:06.893
I can't wait.

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:09.552
- Come on!

00:12:09.552 --> 00:12:11.125
(laughing)

00:12:11.125 --> 00:12:12.160
Yes!
- Woo!

00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:14.710
I am so glad I am not at Chi Spacca

00:12:14.710 --> 00:12:15.580
right now eating this.

00:12:15.580 --> 00:12:17.070
The noises coming out of my mouth-

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:18.635
- We'd be causing a scene.

00:12:18.635 --> 00:12:19.468
- Let's do bone marrow.

00:12:19.468 --> 00:12:21.410
So, the last two pies that we had

00:12:21.410 --> 00:12:23.840
were accompanied by some
of the greatest sauces.

00:12:23.840 --> 00:12:26.680
This one, the sauce comes
right in the middle,

00:12:26.680 --> 00:12:27.980
and you just scoop it out.

00:12:29.215 --> 00:12:30.048
- Whoa.
- Whoa.

00:12:30.048 --> 00:12:30.881
- Get outta here!

00:12:30.881 --> 00:12:32.190
- Bathe my mouth.

00:12:32.190 --> 00:12:34.470
- I never thought that a beef stew

00:12:34.470 --> 00:12:38.600
would need to add more
soft moistness to it.

00:12:38.600 --> 00:12:39.670
It's out of this world.

00:12:39.670 --> 00:12:41.230
- The last thing I want
to say about this...

00:12:41.230 --> 00:12:42.253
Look at that meat.

00:12:43.490 --> 00:12:44.453
- Well, fellas.

00:12:45.410 --> 00:12:47.200
Time to say pie pie for now.

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:50.040
- I became a pie today,
and it's a good life.

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:53.680
- Steven, which savory pie was the most

00:12:53.680 --> 00:12:55.360
worth it to you at its price?

00:12:55.360 --> 00:12:58.120
- All these pies I think tied for me,

00:12:58.120 --> 00:13:00.080
so I had to give it to the best sauce.

00:13:00.080 --> 00:13:02.460
My Worth It winner, I'm
going with Saraba today.

00:13:02.460 --> 00:13:03.500
The onion sauce?

00:13:03.500 --> 00:13:05.210
Holy smokes.

00:13:05.210 --> 00:13:06.043
What about you?

00:13:06.043 --> 00:13:06.876
- Gosh, I love pie.

00:13:06.876 --> 00:13:10.350
Chi Spacca, I think most high end food

00:13:10.350 --> 00:13:12.610
is not as good taken away

00:13:12.610 --> 00:13:14.720
as it would be eaten in the restaurant,

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:16.810
but that pie I think is
a great exactly of one

00:13:16.810 --> 00:13:19.630
that's maybe even better eaten at home.

00:13:19.630 --> 00:13:21.360
My Worth It winner goes to WOODSPOON.

00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:23.080
I love chicken pot pie.

00:13:23.080 --> 00:13:25.150
That pot pie was perfect, and I loved it.

00:13:25.150 --> 00:13:26.708
- Adam, who's your Worth It winner?

00:13:26.708 --> 00:13:27.541
- [Adam] WOODSPOON.

00:13:27.541 --> 00:13:29.630
- By the way, what pants
are you wearing right now?

00:13:29.630 --> 00:13:31.130
'Cause I'm wearing sweatpants because

00:13:31.130 --> 00:13:33.070
I knew this would be a heavy pie day, so.

00:13:33.070 --> 00:13:35.610
- Yeah, I'm wearing
shorts with a drawstring,

00:13:35.610 --> 00:13:37.260
and it is not tied.

00:13:37.260 --> 00:13:39.843
(catchy music)

00:13:41.129 --> 00:13:44.540
♪ Worth It ♪

00:13:44.540 --> 00:13:47.948
♪ Worth It ♪

00:13:47.948 --> 00:13:49.401
♪ Worth It ♪

